<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Byeonhan</h1><p class="st-meta">100 BCE – 42 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederacy of twelve small states on the lower Nakdong river, famed for producing iron that it traded across the Korea Strait and to Chinese commanderies.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Byeonhan, also called Byeonjin, took shape in the 1st century BCE in the southeast of the peninsula as one of the three Samhan confederacies, alongside <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mahan">Mahan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jinhan">Jinhan</a>. Chinese records list twelve member states, sharing customs and language closely with neighbouring Jinhan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each statelet was ruled by its own chief, with walled settlements and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> farming. Its iron was its great asset: Byeonhan supplied iron to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mahan">Mahan</a>, Wa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelang-commandery">Lelang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daifang-commandery">Daifang</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a>, and iron ingots served as currency. Chinese writers also mention the custom of flattening infants' heads and large bird feathers placed in burials.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 1st century CE the Byeonhan statelets developed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gaya-confederacy">Gaya confederacy</a>, traditionally dated to 42 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suro-of-geumgwan-gaya">King Suro</a> founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/geumgwan-gaya">Geumgwan Gaya</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gimhae">Gimhae</a>. Archaeology shows a gradual change rather than a sharp break.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/byeonhan.jpg" alt="Byeonhan" width="320" height="501"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1st_century_Korea_Byeonhan_highlighted.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 BCE – 42 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Byeonjin, Pyonhan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-29&amp;focus=byeonhan" aria-label="Byeonhan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7909 873 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Byeonhan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8247 1162h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8229 1129h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8229 1129h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day South Korea. <a href="/atlas?year=-29&amp;focus=byeonhan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
